    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** (86.26) headlining our offensive attack. His matchup against Zack Littell is the day's premier opportunity; despite the marginal friction caused by the pitcher's efficiency in holding runners, the park factors and the strategic placement of **Corbin Carroll** at the #3 spot in the order provide a ceiling few others can touch. Joining him as a high-conviction play is **Josh Naylor** (78.46), who benefits from favorable wind conditions and a soft matchup against K. Montero. The combination of **Josh Naylor**'s power profile and the +5% park adjustment makes him an essential anchor for our 5x5 production. We are also looking for a significant output from **James Wood** (75.08), whose elite xwOBA metrics and top-of-the-order usage override the concern of facing Eduardo Rodriguez.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Travis Bazzana** (50.93) on the bench. While his position at the top of the lineup and strong platoon advantage against the right-handed Jack Leiter presented a compelling case, he fell just short of the efficiency threshold required to displace our current starters. We opted to lean into the higher-scoring profiles of **Henry Bolte** and **Seiya Suzuki** for their greater power-to-at-bat projections. Investors should note that today is heavily influenced by \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board\u2014players like **Dalton Rushing**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Jose Caballero**, **Brice Turang**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Tyler Soderstrom** have had their scores modeled on historical precedent. Should these confirmed lineups shift, particularly for lower-order hitters like **Alex Freeland**, we will need to monitor the efficiency scores closely to protect our game caps. **Mickey Moniak** remains a non-factor due to his ongoing status on the IL, while the remaining reserves like **Austin Riley**, **Kyle Karros**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **